22	/**
23	 Safelists define what HTML (elements and attributes) to allow through a {@link Cleaner}. Everything else is removed.
24	 <p>
25	 Start with one of the defaults:
26	 </p>
27	 <ul>
28	 <li>{@link #none}
29	 <li>{@link #simpleText}
30	 <li>{@link #basic}
31	 <li>{@link #basicWithImages}
32	 <li>{@link #relaxed}
33	 </ul>
34	 <p>
35	 If you need to allow more through (please be careful!), tweak a base safelist with:
36	 </p>
37	 <ul>
38	 <li>{@link #addTags(String... tagNames)}
39	 <li>{@link #addAttributes(String tagName, String... attributes)}
40	 <li>{@link #addEnforcedAttribute(String tagName, String attribute, String value)}
41	 <li>{@link #addProtocols(String tagName, String attribute, String... protocols)}
42	 </ul>
43	 <p>
44	 You can remove any setting from an existing safelist with:
45	 </p>
46	 <ul>
47	 <li>{@link #removeTags(String... tagNames)}
48	 <li>{@link #removeAttributes(String tagName, String... attributes)}
49	 <li>{@link #removeEnforcedAttribute(String tagName, String attribute)}
50	 <li>{@link #removeProtocols(String tagName, String attribute, String... removeProtocols)}
51	 </ul>
52	
53	 <p>
54	 The {@link Cleaner} and these safelists assume that you want to clean a <code>body</code> fragment of HTML (to add user
55	 supplied HTML into a templated page), and not to clean a full HTML document. If the latter is the case, you could wrap
56	 the templated document HTML around the cleaned body HTML.
57	 </p>
58	 <p>
59	 Safelists are mutable. A {@link Cleaner} uses the supplied safelist directly, so later changes affect later cleaning
60	 calls. If you want to share a safelist across threads, finish configuring it first and do not mutate it while it is in
61	 use. To build a variant from an existing configuration, use {@link #Safelist(Safelist)} to make a copy.
62	 </p>
63	 <p>
64	 If you are going to extend a safelist, please be very careful. Make sure you understand what attributes may lead to
65	 XSS attack vectors. URL attributes are particularly vulnerable and require careful validation. See 
66	 the <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-filter-evasion-cheatsheet">XSS Filter Evasion Cheat Sheet</a> for some
67	 XSS attack examples (that jsoup will safeguard against with the default Cleaner and Safelist configuration).
68	 </p>
69	 */
70	public class Safelist {
71	    private static final String All = ":all";
72	    private static final TagName AllTag = TagName.valueOf(All);
73	    private final Set<TagName> tagNames; // tags allowed, lower case. e.g. [p, br, span]
74	    private final Map<TagName, Set<AttributeKey>> attributes; // tag -> attribute[]. allowed attributes [href] for a tag.
75	    private final Map<TagName, Map<AttributeKey, AttributeValue>> enforcedAttributes; // always set these attribute values
76	    private final Map<TagName, Map<AttributeKey, Set<Protocol>>> protocols; // allowed URL protocols for attributes
